# Release names that already have an owner.
#
# Each line is an image reference PATTERN — a repository suffix, then the tag
# shape — that exactly ONE pipeline is allowed to mint. Today that is
# hanzoai/cloud: the `image` job of its .hanzo/workflows/cicd.yml claims
# refs/tags/v<N> as a compare-and-swap BEFORE it builds, smokes the bytes it
# built, and pins them here itself through pin.sh. Nothing else hands that name
# out, so nothing may be DEPLOYED under it that the owner did not mint.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Nothing else covers a hand-pinned image reaching
# production. pipeline-only-releases.yaml governs PUBLISHING a v* name, not
# pinning a sha- one; pins.py objects in CI, and CI is not what deploys. So a
# sha- tag written by hand into a values file renders and rolls out, carrying
# bytes no pipeline minted, smoked or claimed.
#
# TWO READERS, ONE FACT.
#
#   templates/_helpers.tpl (app.image) refuses to RENDER a pin for one of these
#     repositories unless it names a release and carries a digest. The image
#     reference is formed in exactly one place, so a values file carrying a sha
#     produces no Deployment at all — on a workstation, in universe CI, and at
#     cd.hanzo.ai alike. Production keeps serving the release it was last given,
#     and the Application says in one sentence why it stopped.
#
#   scripts/ci/pins.py refuses to HOLD one of these on the legacy ratchet — a
#     lane that mints versions is never "not yet able to" — and, with --resolve,
#     refuses a pin whose bytes cannot name the commit they were built from. The
#     chart can ask a registry nothing; that read is what catches a v-tag some
#     other lane published back when it still could, which is exactly how
#     production came to report {"revision":"unknown"} about itself.
#
# A THIRD reader cannot read this file. pipeline-only-releases.yaml is a
# ValidatingAdmissionPolicy and CEL has no filesystem, so its `claimed` variable
# holds these same strings inline — and pins.py asserts the two agree character
# for character. One fact, one place, with a proof rather than a convention.
#
# Adding a repository here is DATA, one line, and it asserts that the name now
# has an owner. It is the last step of giving a repo a release lane, never the
# first: a repo whose builds cannot yet mint a version belongs in pins.py's
# LEGACY ratchet instead, which is a queue to be emptied rather than a claim.
/hanzoai/cloud:v[0-9]+[.][0-9]+[.][0-9]+
